---
import Admin from '../../../layouts/Admin.astro';
import { CATEGORIES } from '../../../data/article-meta.js';
---
<Admin title="Writing" active="blog">

  <h1 class="adm-h1">Writing</h1>
  <p class="adm-lede">
    Articles for the Insights section. These are what bring engineers to the
    site from a Google search, which is a slower and far cheaper way to be found
    than advertising.
  </p>

  <p class="ph-status" id="wr-status" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></p>
  <p class="ph-loading" id="wr-loading">Loading…</p>

  <!-- ── the list ── -->
  <section id="wr-list-pane" hidden>
    <div class="ph-have-head">
      <div>
        <h2 class="ph-h2">Articles</h2>
        <p class="ph-count" id="wr-count"></p>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="ph-btn" id="wr-new">Write a new article</button>
    </div>
    <div id="wr-list"></div>
  </section>

  <!-- ── the editor ── -->
  <section id="wr-edit-pane" hidden>
    <div class="ph-have-head">
      <div>
        <h2 class="ph-h2" id="wr-edit-title">New article</h2>
        <p class="ph-count" id="wr-edit-sub"></p>
      </div>
      <button type="button" class="ph-btn-s" id="wr-back">← All articles</button>
    </div>

    <div class="wr-form">
      <label class="ph-field">
        <span>Title</span>
        <input id="wr-title" type="text" maxlength="90" placeholder="Minimum web between pierced holes" />
        <small class="wr-help" id="wr-title-count"></small>
      </label>

      <label class="ph-field">
        <span>Summary — the line under the title in Google results</span>
        <textarea id="wr-desc" rows="2" maxlength="160" placeholder="The rule we use for the web between two pierced holes, why it exists, and what goes wrong on the press when it is ignored."></textarea>
        <small class="wr-help" id="wr-desc-count"></small>
      </label>

      <label class="ph-field">
        <span>Category</span>
        <select id="wr-cat">
          {CATEGORIES.map((c) => <option value={c.id}>{c.id}</option>)}
        </select>
      </label>

      <label class="ph-field">
        <span>The article</span>
        <textarea id="wr-body" class="wr-body" rows="22" placeholder="## The rule&#10;&#10;Write it the way you would explain it on the phone."></textarea>
        <small class="wr-help" id="wr-body-count"></small>
      </label>

      <div class="wr-acts">
        <button type="button" class="ph-btn" id="wr-save">Save draft</button>
        <button type="button" class="ph-btn wr-pub" id="wr-publish">Publish</button>
        <a class="ph-btn-s" id="wr-preview" href="#" target="_blank" rel="noopener" hidden>Preview ↗</a>
        <button type="button" class="ph-btn-s ph-del" id="wr-delete" hidden>Delete</button>
      </div>
      <p class="wr-note" id="wr-preview-note" hidden>
        Preview shows the <b>last saved version</b>, not what is on screen — the
        site has to rebuild first, which takes about two minutes. Save, wait,
        then preview.
      </p>
    </div>

    <details class="wr-md">
      <summary>How to format the article</summary>
      <div class="wr-md-in">
        <p>
          Plain text works. These few marks are the only ones worth knowing —
          the title above is the page heading, so headings inside the article
          start at <code>##</code>.
        </p>
        <table class="wr-md-tbl">
          <tr><td><code>## A heading</code></td><td>A section heading</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>### A smaller heading</code></td><td>Under a section</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>**important**</code></td><td><b>important</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>- a point</code></td><td>A bullet list, one per line</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>1. a step</code></td><td>A numbered list</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>[the calculator](/pcd-calculator/)</code></td><td>A link</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>&gt; a quote</code></td><td>An indented quote</td></tr>
        </table>
        <p>
          Leave a blank line between paragraphs. Linking to your own pages —
          the calculators, a capability page — is worth doing: it keeps readers
          on the site and it is one of the few SEO habits that still clearly
          works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <section class="adm-brief">
    <h2>What is worth writing</h2>
    <p class="adm-brief-lede">
      Write what you would tell a customer on the phone when they ask something
      for the third time that week. That is the article. Nobody searches for
      "quality tooling solutions"; they search for the problem in front of them.
    </p>
    <ul class="adm-list">
      <li><b>"Why is my progressive die cracking at the first station?"</b> — a fault you have actually diagnosed, and what it turned out to be.</li>
      <li><b>Minimum web between pierced holes</b>, with the rule you use and why. You already have the calculator; the article is what sends people to it.</li>
      <li><b>D2 vs OHNS vs EN8</b> — when each is worth the money on a die set, in plain terms.</li>
      <li><b>What to send a tool room with an enquiry</b>, so you get a real quotation back instead of three questions.</li>
      <li><b>A job that went wrong and what it taught you.</b> Riskier, and by some distance the most convincing thing on any tool room's website.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="adm-brief-foot">
      Length does not matter. Six hundred honest words from someone who has
      built the thing beat two thousand written to fill a page — and read as
      though a person wrote them, which is increasingly rare and increasingly
      obvious.
    </p>
  </section>

  <script>
    import '../../../scripts/admin-blog.js';
  </script>

</Admin>
